    {
      "id": "57de8925-6015-4d79-8545-b32bb967f3cd",
      "phaseId": "0e4c21e5-1cf7-4c0a-a00e-76ffbef60703",
      "number": 343,
      "shortId": "DDDV7",
      "priority": 4,
      "shortName": "scrolling-is-observable-from-a",
      "title": "Scrolling is observable from a description too — plus the worked example a reader can copy",
      "status": "deferred",
      "description": "Rust code can already listen to scrolling: `ScrollRegion::on_scroll_start` / `on_scroll` /\n`on_scroll_end`, shipped in F004/P011/T3. This task is only about plugins.\n\n## Decision (with the user, 2026-07-26): wait for the WASM runtime\n\nDo not build this before the plugin runtime exists (F003/P001, plugin-08). Reasons, in order:\n\n1. **Nothing would receive it.** No plugin is running today. The only consumers of a declarative\n   scroll event would be RPC and the app's own screens, and neither needs one.\n2. **We would build the wrong half.** Sending the event out is the awkward part (a name on the wire,\n   arguments, routing it back to the right node). The pleasant part — the author writing a plain\n   function — belongs to the plugin toolkit, which does not exist yet either.\n3. **Nothing in today's work blocks it.** The listeners, the metrics payload and the single\n   chokepoint they fire from are all in place.\n\n## What it should look like when the runtime lands\n\nThe plugin author attaches a function to the scroll area, and that is the whole API. No id, no name,\nno arguments — attaching to the region IS how you know which region fired:\n\n```ts\nScroll({ axes: \"both\", onScrollEnd: (s) => { if (s.offsetY === s.maxY) loadMore() } })\n```\n\nThe user pushed back on an earlier design where the author supplied an identifying argument, and was\nright to: that was machinery from underneath the API surfacing in it. If the API ever makes an author\nname their own scroll area, that is a bug in the API.\n\nUnderneath, the toolkit keeps the function on the plugin's side and gives heca a name for it. heca\nsends that name back when the region scrolls. This is R4 in `docs/chrome-and-ui.md` and is the same\npath `press` and `change` already take — no new mechanism.\n\n## Which of the three events should cross\n\n`scroll_start` and `scroll_end` only. The continuous `scroll` fires on every movement (each wheel\nnotch, each pointer move during a drag), and one message per movement to another program is too many.\n`scroll_end` carries the full position, so anything that needs to know where it stopped gets it. If a\nplugin ever genuinely needs live tracking, decide on a rate limit then rather than guessing one now.\n\n## Still to settle when built\n\n- An action does not describe the arguments it takes (`ActionMeta` has label, icon, policy, confirm —\n  no argument list). So a wrong or misspelled argument is ignored in silence. That is the same shape\n  of defect this phase fixed twice, and it is worth fixing before adding more argument-carrying\n  events.\n- The declarative event vocabulary is documented as three words (`press`, `change`, `toggle`) in both\n  `docs/widgets.md` and `heca/src/chrome/view.rs`. Update both, and say why scrolling earns its own:\n  it is the only widget event with a beginning and an end rather than a single moment.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [
        {
          "id": "57de8925-6015-4d79-8545-b32bb967f3cd-check-001-planned-deferred",
          "date": "2026-07-26T21:17:49.064Z",
          "fromStatus": "planned",
          "toStatus": "deferred",
          "title": "Waits on the WASM plugin runtime (F003/P001). There are no plugins running, so there is nothing to send a scroll event to. Building only the sending half now would add an awkward API with no user, and it would be replaced by the toolkit anyway. Decided with the user 2026-07-26.",
          "description": "Waits on the WASM plugin runtime (F003/P001). There are no plugins running, so there is nothing to send a scroll event to. Building only the sending half now would add an awkward API with no user, and it would be replaced by the toolkit anyway. Decided with the user 2026-07-26."
        }
      ],
