    {
      "id": "787ba99d-c2e9-4dd0-bd28-323ec208c898",
      "phaseId": "0e4c21e5-1cf7-4c0a-a00e-76ffbef60703",
      "number": 398,
      "shortId": "JVM6F",
      "priority": 9,
      "shortName": "plugins-must-be-able-to-build",
      "title": "Plugins must be able to build EVERY widget — no host-only exceptions",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "**Decided by Antonio 2026-08-07**, twice and emphatically: *\"plugins cannot build everything? that's a problem. We need API for plugin to use all widgets.\"* then, on being shown the list: *\"Most of these are essentials for plugins. If they want a progressbar they cant, or a dialog, or flex... WE MUST SUPPORT ALL.\"*\n\n**There is no host-only list. Every widget gets a `WidgetKind`.**\n\n# What is NOT the problem — read this first\n\n**The authoring ergonomics already exist and are good.** `heca-view/src/build.rs` is the typed SwiftUI-style builder (plugin-task-ui-2, done): a type per kind, so `Label::new(\"x\").title(\"y\")` does not compile, children only on kinds that take them, and `every_widget_property_is_reachable_from_the_sdk` fails when a property is unreachable from it.\n\n**The `WidgetKind` enum being closed is also not the problem.** `realize` must turn a kind into a real Rust widget compiled into heca; a plugin cannot ship a Rust type over the wire, so the buildable set is whatever the host binary contains. An open string kind would only turn \"missing\" into \"renders nothing\".\n\n**The problem is coverage, and nothing else: 32 kinds for ~70 exported types.** A plugin writes lovely code and has no `Dialog`, no `ProgressBar`, no `Flex`, no `Menu` to write. Do not re-litigate the enum or the SDK — add the kinds.\n\n# The gap, measured\n\n`heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/mod.rs` exports **70** public types. `WidgetKind` (`heca-view/src/lib.rs:38`) has **32** variants.\n\nStripping the enums/config/aliases (`*Variant`, `Glyph`, `NfGlyph`, `ScrollInfo`, `ScrollAxes`, `MenuAnchor`, `MenuEntry`, `Orientation`, `RegionMode`, `RevealAlign`, `LabelSide`, `DotStatus`, `Ellipsis`, `ActiveMarker`, `GridCell`, `Command`, `Toast*`, `TooltipSide`, `Container` = alias of `Flex`), the **18 widgets with no kind** are:\n\n`Menu` · `MenuItem` · `ContextMenu` · `Dialog` · `Tooltip` · `ProgressBar` · `Spinner` · `CardGrid` · `FocusScope` · `Visibility` · `NfIcon` · `PaneFrame` · `Flex` · `CommandPalette` · `Pane` · `ChromeRegion` · `ScrollRegion` · `ToastStack`\n\n# Why the gap was invisible\n\n`every_widget_kind_realizes_to_a_live_widget_except_the_host_only_ones` walks `WidgetKind::ALL` and proves every declared kind maps to a live widget. **One direction only** — a widget with no kind is invisible to it. That is why AGENTS' \"realize coverage is COMPLETE\" is true and still leaves half the library unreachable: it means \"no kind is a dead end\", not \"every widget is reachable\".\n\n# ⚠️ The settled rule this task supersedes\n\nAGENTS says: *\"A widget whose state is a live host signal is HOST-ONLY — static serializable data cannot drive a signal, so a declarative one would be a dead control. `ScrollBar` is the case (plugins use `Scroll`).\"*\n\nCorrect **for a model that can only carry literals**. It stops being correct once a node can name a value key — **P085(F003)/T360** (`58SE7`), whose step 5 is exactly that. So a declarative `ProgressBar` is not a dead control: its `value` names a key, the plugin writes the key, the bound signal updates in place. **The host-only category was a missing mechanism, not a property of the widgets.**\n\n**T360's binding spelling is DECIDED (2026-08-04, Antonio) — do not re-ask, and do not repeat the old \"open question\" line:**\n\n```rust\nstruct ViewNode {\n    kind, props,\n    events:   Map<EventName, Intent>,   // already exists — a name → an indirection\n    bindings: Map<PropName, String>,    // prop name → value key. THE SAME SHAPE.\n    children,\n}\n```\n\nA `bindings` map beside `events`, because the node already expresses this indirection for behaviour. `PropValue` is left alone. The literal in `props` is the **fallback**, shown until the plugin's first write. `realize` reads the literal, then lets a binding override it via `container_value(mount, key)`. **Rejected:** `PropValue::Text(\"@key\")` (a sigil collides with a legitimate `@handle`) and `PropValue::Binding(String)` (grows the wire vocabulary; kept only as a fallback if two maps prove worse).\n\n**Consequence: T360 is a hard dependency for the signal-driven widgets** (`ProgressBar`, `ScrollBar`, `ScrollRegion`, `CommandPalette`, `ToastStack`, `Pane`, `ChromeRegion`). The rest need nothing and go first.\n\n**Update the AGENTS ⭐ block when this lands**, so the host-only rule is not re-applied by a later session reading a superseded line.\n\n# What to build\n\n1. **A `WidgetKind` + `realize` arm for every widget in the list**, following the settled process: *\"Extending the vocabulary is host-side work (widget + `realize` arm + showcase + `docs/widgets.md`); plugins only compose existing kinds.\"*\n2. **Guard the OTHER direction** — the reason this will not need redoing. A test that walks every widget `heca-grid-ui` exports and **fails if it has no `WidgetKind`**. Today a widget can be added with no kind and nothing notices. Mechanism open (an inventory the crate exposes, a macro-collected list, a source-walking test); the requirement is that it cannot be forgotten.\n3. **Showcase + `docs/widgets.md`** for every new kind. The SDK follows automatically.\n\n# Order of work\n\n**First — `Menu` / `MenuItem`**, the case that surfaced this and the one T395 is settling. `heca-view` has **no menu concept at all** (no kind, no `context_menu` prop; `heca-view-realize` never touches one), so a plugin's own row opens **nothing** on right-click: the same silence as the missing `.nav_key(..)` that motivated P084 — no error, no failing test.\n\nShape, using conventions that already exist — **no new mechanism**:\n- `WidgetKind::Menu` + `WidgetKind::MenuItem`.\n- Attach with the settled slot convention (*\"a widget with several places for children takes a `slot` prop on the CHILD\"*): `slot: \"context_menu\"`. **Not** a menus map, **not** a second children vector.\n- A composed row is just `children` on the `MenuItem` node; behaviour is `on_press → Intent`.\n- `realize` fills the existing `Base::context_menu` slot (`heca-grid-ui/src/component.rs:238`, `Fn() -> ContextMenu`) with a closure that re-realizes the described menu per opening. **One slot, both paths.**\n\n**Symmetry worth keeping:** `MenuItem::child` takes a builder closure only because native code must rebuild an owned subtree per opening. A `ViewNode` is data and re-realizable by construction, so the declarative path gets composed rows **with no closure at all**.\n\n**Then** the ones needing nothing new: `Dialog`, `Tooltip`, `Spinner`, `CardGrid`, `PaneFrame`, `Flex`, `Visibility`, `FocusScope`.\n\n**Last** the signal-driven ones, behind T360: `ProgressBar`, `ScrollRegion`, `CommandPalette`, `ToastStack`, `Pane`, `ChromeRegion`.\n\n# Constraints (settled — do not re-open)\n\n- `heca-grid-ui` never learns about `ViewNode`; the dependency runs app → library only.\n- Behaviour crosses as an `Intent` (action id + args), never a callback.\n- A slot/child is `impl Component` — never narrowed to a closed enum.\n- A realized subtree enters a widget via a `*_boxed` setter (`Dialog::body_boxed` is the precedent), because `Box<dyn Component>` is not itself `Component`.\n\n# Edge cases\n\n- `Flex`/`Container` may already be covered by `VStack`/`HStack` — check before adding a third spelling of the same box.\n- `Visibility` and `FocusScope` are wrappers, not visuals — decide whether they are kinds or **props** on the wrapped node (a prop is probably right and cheaper). Either way a plugin must be able to express them.\n- `NfIcon` needs glyph-by-name — see **T397** (the generated name↔`Glyph` mapping).\n- Adding a kind is not free: `WidgetKind::ALL`, the drift guard (`heca-grid-ui/tests/prop_drift.rs`), the SDK's reachability test and the showcase all walk the vocabulary.\n- `Pane` and `ChromeRegion` wrap app concepts (a terminal surface, a chrome region). Check what a described one would mean before forcing a kind on them — but the default is a kind, and \"it is awkward\" is not a reason to leave a hole.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "",
