      "createdAt": "2026-08-07T09:52:42.744Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-08-07T09:57:38.763Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "5d3bdfaa-9b33-47b1-94e7-35e315ad55a6",
      "phaseId": "0e4c21e5-1cf7-4c0a-a00e-76ffbef60703",
      "number": 399,
      "shortId": "6P6HQ",
      "priority": 10,
      "shortName": "on-peek-a-capability-is-one-bu",
      "title": "on_peek — a capability is one builder on the widget (kill hint_target + the host registry)",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "**Decided by Antonio 2026-08-07.** He sketched the shape he wants for every capability:\n\n```rust\nlet container = FocusableContainer::new().child(topBar).on_peek(|| decide here what to do)\n// \"FocusableContainer is just a name. an example. EVERYONE CAN use it with no effort.\"\n```\n\nand, on the rule being stated back: *\"this is how i want you to work.\"* It is written in **`AGENTS.md` → ⭐⭐ RULE ZERO** and outranks the architecture section: *\"I want everything we build to be available for whoever wants to build a plugin or contribute to the project. THIS IS THE FIRST MOST IMPORTANT RULE.\"*\n\n# The defect — a shipped feature plugins cannot have\n\n`prefix+/` (the universal hint picker) is registered like this today:\n\n```rust\nlet id = hints.register(InteractionIntent::FocusPaneThenAction { … });  // heca/src/chrome/mod.rs:851\nbutton.hint_target(id)                                                   // …and carry an id around\n```\n\nThree things a caller must know: a registry exists, they must pre-register, and `InteractionIntent` — which is **`pub(crate)`** (`heca/src/app/interaction.rs:99`).\n\n**There are two doors, and they are not equal:**\n\n| door | takes | who can use it |\n|---|---|---|\n| `HintTargetRegistry::register` (`heca/src/chrome/mod.rs:2808`) | `InteractionIntent` | **host only** (crate-private enum) |\n| `HintTargets::register` (`heca-view-realize/src/lib.rs:69`) | `Intent` (name + args) | anyone, incl. plugins |\n\nThe top bar (`chrome/mod.rs:1816`) and the pane header (`:851`) use the **first**. A plugin can construct **no** variant of `InteractionIntent`, so it can only register a plain `Intent` naming an action — and a component's selection-dependent actions declare `ActionPolicy::ContainerFocused`, which **refuses** them unless the container already holds the keyboard. `FocusContainerThenAction` exists precisely to satisfy that, and a plugin cannot say it. So a plugin's row hint is blocked with no way to express the fix: a second-class version of a shipped feature.\n\n# What to build\n\n**`on_peek` as a universal slot on `Base`**, written with `EventExt` like `on_click` / `nav_key` / `context_menu`:\n\n```rust\nRow::new().child(Label::new(&pane.name)).on_peek(move || cursor_to(row_id));\n```\n\nThe framework owns everything behind it: collecting targets from the tree (`collect_hint_targets` already walks `Base`), assigning the letter, drawing it, and firing the handler on a pick. No id, no registry, no host type at a call site.\n\n**Then the declarative half**, which is the point of the rule: `on_peek` is an event like `on_press`, so `ViewNode.events` maps it to an `Intent` and `realize` wires it. A plugin writes the same line in a described tree and gets identical behaviour. **One door — delete the other.**\n\n# What this replaces (delete, do not leave beside)\n\n- `Base::hint_target(id)` and `NavExt`/`HintExt`'s id-passing form.\n- `HintTargetRegistry` in the app (`heca/src/chrome/mod.rs:2800`-ish) and `ViewHintTargets` (`:2848`).\n- `crate::chrome::named_press` (`:2877`) — **the specific conflation that caused the bug below**.\n- `BuildCx.hints` threading through every builder that only exists to carry the registry.\n\n# The bug that exposed it (do not lose this)\n\n`prefix+/` on a sidebar row **activates the pane and leaves the sidebar**. Antonio: *\"it did not activate the pane just focus the cursor above the letter i peek. Currently it activates the pane.\"*\n\nCause: commit **`e712d70`** (2026-07-30, \"a row is named by its component, and its click is an action\") replaced `hints.register(InteractionIntent::FocusPane { pane_id })` with `named_press(pane_row_press(pane_id), …)` — pointing the hint target at the row's **click** intent. A click on a row means *go there and leave*; a hint pick means something else. One intent serving two gestures.\n\n**What he wants for the workspaces dock** (his words, 2026-08-07): keep the letters over every pane/workspace row, and *\"i want it to focus the cursor in the peeked letter and is good if the pane gets active\"* — i.e. **stay in the sidebar**. That is exactly `workspaces.peek_selected` (`providers/workspaces/mod.rs:218`, dispatched at `:307` as `activate(cx, Activate::AndStay)`), whose own tests already say *\"peek still brings the pane to the front\"* (`:1740`) and *\"peek leaves the container holding the keyboard\"* (`:1749`).\n\nTwo things are missing to aim it at a row:\n1. **A `key` argument on the peek action** — it acts on `tree().current_item()` (the cursor) and cannot be told which row. Declared in the component's own `actions()`, handled in its own `perform` (`cx.set_selected(key)` then the existing peek). Optional, defaulting to the cursor.\n2. **The rows registering peek instead of the click** — `providers/workspaces/mod.rs:726` and `:1187`.\n\n⚠️ **Do NOT do this with `FocusContainerThenAction`.** That was my proposal on 2026-08-07 and it is exactly what RULE ZERO forbids — a `pub(crate)` composite a plugin cannot construct. If \"focus my container, then run my action\" is needed, it must exist **as a named action** a plugin can name, like `focus_pane` and `unfocus_dock` do.\n\n# Where the letter is drawn\n\nAntonio first suggested one letter over the whole dock, then settled on keeping per-row letters and letting the component decide the reaction: *\"we can also leave as it is now, showing peek letters above all panes/ws and let the component decide how to react.\"* So placement is unchanged — this task is about **what a pick does** and **who can declare it**.\n\n# Verification\n\nHeadless tests for the slot and the declarative mapping, but the real check is Antonio: `prefix+/`, pick a sidebar row's letter → the cursor lands on that row, the pane goes active, **the keyboard stays in the sidebar**. Plus the top bar and pane-header buttons still pick. **Do not claim it works** — the whole class of bug here was invisible to a green suite.\n\n# Related\n\n- **T395** — `.context_menu()` is the precedent this copies: it replaced `context_path` + a builder registry + a `nav_key` nobody remembered.\n- **T398** — plugins must be able to build every widget; same rule, applied to the vocabulary.\n- The second lookup (`open_for_nav_key` beside `open_for_focused`) is the same defect in the keyboard path and should die with this: one notion of where the keyboard is.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "",
      "completedAt": "",
      "createdAt": "2026-08-07T15:18:05.911Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-08-07T15:18:05.911Z"
    }
  ],
  "createdAt": "2026-07-26T16:49:11.138Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-08-07T15:18:05.911Z",
  "handoff": "# P084 handoff — 2026-08-07 · T394 done, T395 mid-flight with a **re-decided API**\n\n## State\n\nBranch `feat/palette-modes`, commit **`3536ec2`** — everything below is **committed** (Antonio asked;\nhis standing rule is otherwise never commit unasked). **1228 tests, clippy 0 warnings** (the only\nline is `block v0.1.6`'s future-incompat note, from a dependency).\n\n- **`T394` (X7SSB) — DONE**, built and **driven by Antonio in the GPU app** (\"its good\"). Its\n  completion summary has the full record; do not re-derive it.\n- **`T395` (BLRSH) — in-progress.** The API was **re-decided by Antonio on 2026-08-07 across a long\n  conversation**. Everything in this handoff wins over the task description, which is older.\n\n---\n\n# 🔴 THE DECIDED MENU API — do not re-open, do not \"improve\"\n\nAntonio rejected three of my designs in a row before this one. What he rejected, so nobody rebuilds\nit: `path` / `target` / `about` on the menu; `alter_menus` / `MenuPath::declare` / `MenuRow::at`;\n`fn context_menus() -> Vec<…>` (*\"why a function that return vec?? still the same as before.. WHY??\ni want simple APIs\"*); and a `MenuPanel` type I invented and he never asked for (*\"YOU HAVE TO ASK\nAND NOT TO INVENT\"*).\n\n## The four names — his, verbatim\n\n```\nMenuItem\nMenu            (container of MenuItem)\nContextualMenu  (contains Menu) shown by right-click or keybinding (triggered event)\nMenuBar         (contains Menu) shown by mouse or keybinding        ← not built yet\n```\n\nThere is no fifth type. The panel is `ContextMenu`'s own body, not a separate name.\n\n> **Naming note for whoever builds it:** he writes `ContextualMenu`; the type in the tree today is\n> `ContextMenu`. Ask him which he wants before renaming — do not decide it.\n\n## What you write\n\n```rust\nlet ctx = ContextualMenu::new(\"pane-menu\").child(\n    Menu::new(\"Pane\", \"What you can do with this pane\")\n        // sugar form\n        .child(MenuItem::new().label(\"Rename\").icon(Glyph::Pencil).on_click(move || rename(id)))\n        // composed form — any widget subtree, with props\n        .child(MenuItem::new().child(\n            Grid::new().prop(\"gap\", 10)\n                .child(Icon::new(Glyph::Trash))\n                .child(Label::new(\"Close\")))),\n);\n\nRow::new()\n    .child(Label::new(&pane.name))\n    .context_menu(ctx);      // ← right-click AND prefix+> / Shift+F10, anchored automatically\n```\n\n## The rules behind it\n\n1. **`MenuItem` takes composed children AND `.label()`/`.icon()` sugar.** His words: *\"composed but\n   also can provide a simpler api with label and icon prop\"*. Children win when both are given —\n   the same precedence `Button` already has.\n2. **One `Menu` per `ContextualMenu`**, several `MenuItem`s in it. He was asked whether a\n   ContextualMenu could hold several Menus (sections): *\"No. Only one menu that contains severl\n   MenuItem\"*.\n3. **`Menu` is content and knows nothing about triggers, anchors or keys.** That is what keeps\n   `MenuBar` a presenter rather than a rewrite: it will show **the same `Menu` value** with its own\n   trigger and keybinding.\n4. **`ContextualMenu::new(\"name\")` — the name is an id**, not a title (`Menu` carries the title and\n   description). Confirmed by him. It is what something else refers to the menu by.\n5. **Two roads to show it, and the declaration is implemented in terms of the explicit one:**\n   - `.context_menu(ctx)` on a widget — covers the two standard triggers. **This exists because the\n     keyboard needs it**: with the menu only inside a closure, `prefix+>` / `Shift+F10` has nothing\n     to find. He worked this out himself: *\"so thats' why you were useing the contextual_menu props\n     on widgets.. mhh i think we need it if so.\"*\n   - `ctx.show(ev)` from any handler — for a trigger you invent (a left click, a long press):\n     `Button::new(\"More\").on_click(move |ev| ctx.show(ev))`.\n6. **The anchor is never the author's, and it comes from the event.** His idea, and it is better\n   than the ambient lookup I proposed: `show(ev)` reads the anchor out of the event — *\"what if\n   shot(event). and the event tranposrt mouse coordinate and the widget that contains the ctx?\"* So\n   a pointer event gives the cursor, a keyboard event gives the target widget's bounds. **This means\n   adding `target_bounds()` to the event** (already in his own sketch in the task description:\n   `menu().show_under(ev.target_bounds())`). No event in flight ⇒ no `show` ⇒ nothing to guess.\n7. **`ContextualMenu` and `Menu` are cheap to clone** (`Rc` inside), so one menu can be declared on\n   several rows and captured by handlers. He left this to me.\n8. **`.name()` on `Menu` is optional** and is the *only* survivor of the contribution design: it\n   lets another component add rows to a menu it does not own. **No `about`** — dropped on his\n   instruction (*\"no about (i really didn't get whats its purpose)\"*). Consequence, recorded so\n   nobody is surprised: a contributed row has no per-row payload, so it acts on app state rather\n   than on the row the menu was opened for. One additive builder later **if** it ever matters —\n   do not pre-build it.\n9. **Known, accepted cost:** with the menu in a closure, nothing in the tree *says* a widget has one\n   the way `nav_key` does — a debug overlay or a test cannot ask. Flagged to him; he did not want a\n   slot back for it.\n\n---\n\n# What is already built (committed in `3536ec2`)\n\n## T394 — the event vocabulary (done, driven)\n\n`heca-grid-ui/src/event.rs` + `src/pointer.rs`. One host event (`Event::Raw(RawPointer)`) resolved\nonce into `PointerDown/Up/Move`, `PointerEnter/Leave`, `Click/DoubleClick/TripleClick`,\n`RightClick/MiddleClick`, `PointerDownOutside`, `Scroll` (with a position), the seven drag events,\n`Focus`/`Blur`, `Mount`/`Unmount`, and `TextInput` separate from `Key`. `EventExt` puts a handler on\nany widget. `Base::one_click_target` claims the **primary button only**. Three new `Component` hooks:\n`hit_bounds`, `after_subtree`, and capture-on-press. Tests: `tests/pointer_routing.rs` (23),\n`tests/pointer_delivery.rs` (12). Docs: rustdoc throughout (including a long `Handled` entry he asked\nfor), `docs/widgets.md` event-model section, an AGENTS.md SETTLED block.\n\n## T395 — first half\n\n- `Menu` (title, description, optional `name`, items, `into_panel`, `extend`);\n  `MenuItem::new(label).on_click(f)` + icon/key/shortcut/danger/enabled; `MenuEntry` alias.\n- `ContextMenu` — the widget that holds the rows and shows them (paints, hit-tests, navigates,\n  dismisses); `MenuAnchor::{At, Under}` with `MenuAnchor::open(panel)`.\n- `Base::context_menu: Option<Box<dyn Fn() -> Menu>>`; `EventExt::context_menu(menu)` /\n  `context_menu_built(closure)` — universal, like `nav_key`.\n- `heca-grid-ui/src/menu.rs`: `install_menu_sink(|menu, anchor|)`, `menu::show`, bubbling to the\n  nearest declaration (`menu_on`), `open_for_focused` for the keyboard trigger.\n- Router: an **unclaimed** `RightClick` opens the nearest declared menu — a widget answering its own\n  right-click still wins.\n- App: sink installed in `startup.rs`, queued on `AppState::pending_menus`, drained once a frame by\n  `chrome::drain_pending_menus` (top of `render_frame`) into `chrome::overlay::present_menu`, which\n  merges contributions for a **named** menu and mounts via `insert_menu_layer` (shared with\n  `open_dropdown`). `ContextMenu::after_select` closes the layer, so an item stays a plain closure.\n- `heca-grid-ui/tests/context_menu_declaration.rs` — 8 tests, one per rule.\n\n**Mismatches with the decided API, to fix first:** `MenuItem` has no composed-children form or\n`.label()`; there is no `ContextualMenu`/`ContextMenu::new(name).child(menu)` shape; `show(ev)` does\nnot exist (only `menu::show(menu, anchor)`); events have no `target_bounds()`.\n\n---\n\n# What is left, in order\n\n1. **Bring the code to the decided API**: `MenuItem` composed content + `.label()`/`.icon()` sugar;\n   the `ContextualMenu::new(id).child(menu)` shape; `show(ev)` with the anchor read from the event;\n   `target_bounds()` on the event. **Composed items rewrite `ContextMenu`'s paint and hit-test** —\n   rows are drawn by hand today. He wants to see that on screen before it is called done.\n2. **Port the workspaces provider's three row menus** to the declaration form\n   (`heca/src/providers/workspaces/mod.rs`: `build_pane_row_menu` ~:1286 and the column/workspace\n   equivalents). Each row has its id where it is built — capture it; no key strings.\n3. **Root-level menu for empty sidebar space** (\"New workspace\") via bubbling — no empty-space\n   hit-test.\n4. **Delete**: `context_path` (~:311), the `menu(id, builder)` registrations (~:300),\n   `MENU_PANE`/`MENU_COLUMN`/`MENU_WORKSPACE` (~:1263), `open_row_context_menu` (`mouse.rs` ~:156)\n   and its position archaeology.\n5. **Generalise `open_context_menu`** from \"the focused pane's menu\" to \"the focused widget's,\n   bubbling\" (`heca_grid_ui::open_for_focused`), and add `Shift+F10` + `Menu` as bindings on the\n   **same** catalogued action in `keybindings.default.toml` (it already has `prefix+>`).\n   Deliberately **not** a `[keys.widgets]` entry — `Escape` being both WM `close_overlay` and widget\n   `dismiss` is the split that swallowed `q`.\n6. **Docs**: the four names, both forms of `MenuItem`, the two roads to show a menu, the anchors,\n   the host sink, a plugin author's minimal example — rustdoc **and** `docs/widgets.md`.\n7. **Hand Antonio a build and WAIT**: right-click a pane, a column, a workspace, empty sidebar\n   space, plus `prefix+>` and `Shift+F10`.\n\n## Then, in his order\n\n- **`Button` composed content / ViewNode** — *\"we need to also rewrite that button to be a ViewNode\n  at the end but first handle this\"*.\n- **DnD**: `P079(F004)/T318` (rewritten to **delete** `DragSurfaceId` and the per-surface machinery,\n  not extend it) and `T396` (the drag ghost + insertion line move into `heca-grid-ui`). `T330` is\n  rejected — no enum left to open. Cause of \"the right sidebar does not drag\": the early return at\n  `heca/src/mouse.rs` (`if point_in_right_sidebar(..) { …; return None; }`) runs before the drag\n  question, and `DragSurfaceId::LeftSidebar` is hardcoded in five files.\n\n---\n\n# How Antonio works — read this before writing a line\n\n- **He is discussing until he says otherwise.** I started editing while he was still asking\n  questions and he stopped me: *\"wait — we are discussing\"*. Answer, then wait for an explicit go.\n- **Ask, never invent.** Inventing `MenuPanel` because his taxonomy \"had no name for it\" cost a\n  round of anger. If a fifth concept seems necessary, that is a question, not a decision.\n- **Never commit unless asked** (he asked for `3536ec2` explicitly).\n- **N copies of a rule = a missing API. Fix the model, not the call site.** He caught me writing the\n  same `Event::PointerDown(_) => Handled::Yes` into nine widgets *while fixing that exact class of\n  bug*; it became `Base::one_click_target`.\n- **He rejects ceremony on sight**: a trait method returning a `Vec`, a registration struct, a\n  builder registry. If an API needs a paragraph of justification, it is wrong.\n- **Do not silently deviate from a shape he sketched.** When his example did not compile as written,\n  the *type* changed to match the sketch, and the deviation was flagged.\n- **He drives every visual check and finds what tests cannot.** Green tests said nothing about six\n  input regressions in one session; his screenshots found all of them. **Do not claim it works.**\n",
